    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "About 40 years ago, satellites began looking down on the Antarctic Peninsula. They only saw a few small "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b1",
            "number": 1
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " of vegetation. These plants covered about 740 square metres—less than a football field."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Since then, "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b2",
            "number": 2
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", the region has been turning green quickly. A new study shows that mosses have taken up more than 12 million square metres. That’s nearly four times the "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b3",
            "number": 3
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " of New York’s Central Park!"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "The study was based on satellite images from 1986 to 2021. It shows that greening has "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b4",
            "number": 4
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " by 30 per cent since 2016. That’s fast enough to cover nearly 75 football fields per year."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "“Our findings show that the impact of climate change has no limit in its "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b5",
            "number": 5
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ",” said Thomas Roland, lead author of the study. “Even in the most remote and "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "isolated",
            "zh": "与世隔绝的"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " region, the landscape is "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b6",
            "number": 6
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ".” Roland is an environmental scientist at the University of Exeter."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "The Antarctic is the coldest place on Earth. But it has recently been "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b7",
            "number": 7
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " by extreme heat events. This summer, a record-breaking heat wave "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b8",
            "number": 8
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " parts of the continent. Temperatures were about 10℃ higher than normal in mid-July."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "As the planet "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b9",
            "number": 9
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", Antarctica will continue to warm, scientists say. That is to say, this greening is only likely to happen faster."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p7",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "As the peninsula becomes greener, more soil will "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b10",
            "number": 10
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". Invasive plants will be more likely to "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "take root",
            "zh": "扎根"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " in the region. Antarctica’s native wildlife and ecosystem can be "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b11",
            "number": 11
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " as a result."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p8",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "“Seeds and small pieces of plants can "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "readily",
            "ref": "v-readily"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " find their way there on the boots of tourists and researchers. They may also "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b12",
            "number": 12
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " through more traditional "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "routes",
            "zh": "途径"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "—birds and the wind,” Roland said."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p9",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "The plant cover could reduce the Antarctic’s "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b13",
            "number": 13
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " to "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "reflect",
            "ref": "v-reflect"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " the sun’s heat energy into space. That’s because darker surfaces take in more heat."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p10",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "These impacts would likely remain "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b14",
            "number": 14
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". At present, the Antarctic landscape is still mostly snow, ice, and rock. In the long run, however, they could further "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "promote",
            "ref": "v-promote"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " plant "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b15",
            "number": 15
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " as the climate continues to warm. “The landscape on the continent could be changed forever,” said Olly Bartlett, another study author."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "i1",
        "type": "imageRef",
        "image": "img1"
      }
    ]
  },
